# Engineering standards

.NET 9, ASP.NET Core, Clean Architecture, Clean Code, and the design patterns already in the solution.

## Clean Architecture

- Domain has **zero** project references. Invariants live on entities (`User.CreateTrader`, `Balance.Debit`). Throw `DomainException`.
- Application orchestrates via MediatR; FluentValidation on commands/queries; no EF, SQL, or ASP.NET types.
- Contracts hold DTOs + interfaces (`IUserRepository`, `IPasswordHasher`). Infra implements them.
- API is the composition root: thin controllers, JWT, middleware, `AddApplication` / `AddPersistence`.
- Handlers never return persistence entities; map to DTOs.

## Clean Code

- Names match existing language (`UserName`, `RecordLogin`, `ConflictException`).
- Small, single-purpose methods. Prefer domain methods over logic in handlers/controllers.
- No commented-out code. No comments that narrate what the code already says.

## Patterns in use (prefer these)

CQRS + MediatR pipeline, Repository + Unit of Work, Factory methods on aggregates, Adapter (Infra), Options pattern, Result-via-exceptions (`DomainException`, `NotFoundException`, `ConflictException`).

## ASP.NET

- Minimal controllers: validate HTTP shape, `IMediator.Send`, map to status codes.
- Keep auth, CORS, OpenAPI, and exception middleware in the API host — not in Core.
- Secrets stay out of source; do not weaken JWT/password hashing.
